<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Sloboda Ukraine</h1><p class="st-meta">1652 CE – 1765 CE (approx.) · capital Kharkiv</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A frontier land of free Cossack settlements east of the Dnieper, founded by refugees and organised into regiments under the tsars.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>From the 1630s, and especially after the wars that followed 1648, Ukrainian peasants and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> fled from Polish-ruled lands into the empty steppe borderland of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/tsardom-of-russia">Tsardom of Russia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> granted them freedom from taxes and land in return for guarding its southern frontier. Their settlements, called slobody, gave the region its name, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharkiv">Kharkiv</a> was founded around 1654.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The region was organised into five <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> regiments, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharkiv">Kharkiv</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumy">Sumy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okhtyrka">Okhtyrka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/izium">Izium</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogozhsk">Ostrogozhsk</a>, each led by a colonel. Unlike the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate</a>, there was no <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hetman">hetman</a>, and the regiments answered directly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. The settlers farmed the black earth, traded and defended the frontier against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-tatars">Crimean Tatar</a> raids. The philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hryhorii-skovoroda">Hryhorii Skovoroda</a> taught at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharkiv-collegium">Kharkiv Collegium</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1765 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine II</a> abolished the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> regiments and created the Sloboda <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a>, turning the Cossacks into ordinary subjects. The region later formed the core of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharkiv">Kharkiv</a> province of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sloboda-ukraine.jpg" alt="Sloboda Ukraine" width="320" height="212"><figcaption>Image: Serhii BobokIf you have any questions please contact with me. Other photos see here.Якщо у Вас є якісь запитання будь... · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sviatohirsk_Lavra_-_05.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1652 CE – 1765 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kharkiv</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ukrainian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Ukraine, Russia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Slobozhanshchyna, Sloboda Cossack regiments</dd></dl></aside></div>
